{% render "partials/_architecture_framing" %}

You are extracting structured features from an unstructured project
description. Your output is the first layer of handles the entire
generation chain builds on — the requirements pass downstream reads
each feature's intent to identify the system-level obligations it
implies, so name specific capabilities, not categories.

**Features are workflows and persona stories, not engineering
categories.** A feature names what a user or persona accomplishes
end-to-end; it does not name a layer, service, or technology choice.

* Features: "a new customer completes onboarding end-to-end", "an
  admin flags suspicious activity and removes it".
* Not features — these are sysarch concerns, redistributed as
  responsibilities and later assigned to components: "storage layer",
  "API gateway", "authentication service", "caching layer".
* Not features — these are implementation details, decided no earlier
  than comparch: "use Redis for sessions", "Postgres table design",
  "GraphQL vs REST".

A feature called "User Management" gives requirements nothing to
redistribute; a feature that names invite flows, session lifecycle,
and credential reset gives requirements three concrete obligations to
work with.

# Input

{{ project_doc }}

# Output format

Emit `<introduction>` (required — 2 to 5 paragraphs: what this project
fundamentally is, which user goals shape the decomposition axis, which
tensions or ambiguities you noticed; a memo to your future self for
when this tier regenerates with feedback), then `<features>`, then
optionally `<vocabulary>`.

Inside `<features>`, group related features under `<group>` blocks
where that aids scannability; standalone features sit directly under
`<features>`. Each `<feature>` has exactly one `<name>` and one
`<intent>`, optionally followed by a self-closing `<implicit/>` marker.
Each `<group>` has one `<name>` and one or more `<feature>` children;
groups do not nest.

# Rules

* `<name>` (on a feature) is 2 to 5 words, title case, naming what the
  feature does for the user — not the engineering category it sits
  in. "Password Reset" beats "Credential Management"; if the name
  could label any SaaS product's marketing page unmodified, it's too
  generic.
* `<intent>` is 2 to 5 sentences describing what the feature does and
  why, in terms specific enough that requirements can extract concrete
  obligations. "Users can pay for things" gives requirements nothing;
  naming the billing cycle, invoice delivery, and the failed-payment
  retry schedule gives it three distinct obligations.
* **Implicit features.** Mark `<implicit/>` on anything the project
  obviously needs that the input doc didn't explicitly name —
  authentication wherever there are user accounts, password reset
  wherever there's login, notifications wherever there are
  asynchronous events. Inferring these is core to this tier's job.
* Feature names are unique across the whole `<features>` block —
  downstream tiers reference features by name.
* Aim for breadth, not depth; each feature gets its own detailed
  decomposition at requirements and below. Do not fabricate
  constraints the input doesn't imply.
* Output only `<introduction>`, `<features>`, and optionally
  `<vocabulary>` — no meta-commentary about what you're doing.

# Vocabulary (optional)

Scan the input for terms the project uses in a project-specific sense
— any phrase a generic reader would get subtly wrong without a
definition. If you find any, emit `<vocabulary>` after `<features>`,
containing one or more `<term name="..." scope="project|feature"
feature-name="...">` entries (`feature-name` required exactly when
`scope="feature"`, and must match an exact feature name declared
above). **Flag the candidate only** — a bare `<term>` naming the word
and its scope, nothing else. The vocab tier's own generation pass
writes the definition, disambiguation, and see-also content; this
tier's job is noticing that a term needs defining, not defining it.
Omit the block entirely rather than padding it if nothing in the
project is genuinely load-bearing terminology.
